    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d612">

                <pb facs="0847" n="833" xml:id="pg833"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5151/981: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-11T12:00:00+07:00">January 11,
                            1944—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received January 11—8:06 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>58. To Secretary of Treasury from Adler, re your 15, January 3.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>I fully agree with your (2). Appropriate Chinese institution for
                        consultation is Commission for Control of Foreign Exchange Assets to which
                        Chinese staff board is being transferred. It is most unlikely that any
                        Chinese Government body consulted in connection with type of transaction
                        referred to in your (3) would take a stiff attitude. Presumably unless
                        satisfactory arrangement is worked out with Chinese authorities for covering
                        U. S. Government agencies’ <hi rendition="#italic">fapi</hi> requirements,
                        more liberal licensing policy referred to in your (2) would also be applied
                        to U. S. Government agencies’ transactions. As you know, U. S. Government
                        civilian agencies in China acting in accordance with instructions from
                        Washington have been selling U. S. currency in black market for more than a
                        month now while American soldiers have been financing their personal
                        expenditures by the same means for the past 2 years.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Re your 1690, November 24.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d612-f1"/> British have
                        not yet given 30 days’ notice of termination required under 1941
                        Sino-British agreement. Shall I submit my resignation as of January
                        31?</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Your 658, May 24<anchor n="26" xml:id="d612-f2"/> and my TF–138, July
                            15.<anchor n="27" xml:id="d612-f3"/> Ministry of Finance has recently
                        asked me for your reaction to Kung’s alternative plan. [Adler.]</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
